      "updated": 1775236990416,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can we ensure short urls are unique?\n\nRequirements:\nShort codes are unique.\nShort codes to be as short as possible\nShort codes are efficiently generated.\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Long Url Prefix\n\n→ Use a fixed prefix of the input url as the short code\n→ Take first N (eg: 8) characters of url and use as short code.\nwww.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/  In this case www.short.ly/www.link\n→ We can maintain a PrecomputedFeed table & query from this instead of Follow and\nPost tables, for the GET /feeds\n\nChallenges\n→ Fails uniqueness constraint\nwww.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/ & www.linkedin.com/in/stefanmai/ both\nmap to www.short.ly/www.link\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Hash Function\n\n→ Random number generator (RNG) to create short codes. Generates random number\neach time a new URL is shortened & used as short code\n→ Can use common RNG fns like JS's Math.random() or more robust cryptographic RNG\nfns for increased unpredictability [create note]\n→ RNGs don't provide enough entropy to ensure short codes are unique\n\n→ Use a hash function like SHA-256 to generate a fixed-size hash code instead\n→ Pure hash functions are deterministic: long URL always maps to same short code\nwithout needing to query DB\n→ May not be desirable (multiple codes per URL or want to prevent\nguessability/adversarial preimages), For latter, can add a secret salt or nonce (HMAC)\n→ Take the output and encode it using a base62 encoding scheme & take first N chars\nas short code. N is determined based on the number of characters needed to minimize\ncollisions (e.g., 8 characters gives 62^8 ≈ 218 trillion possible codes).\n\n\nChallenges\n→ There's still chance of generating duplicate short codes as the number of stored\nURLs increases.\n→ At large scale this can become non-negligible, requiring retries and database checks\nto enforce uniqueness\n\n→ To reduce collisions, we need higher entropy, which means generating longer short\ncodes. However, longer codes negate the benefit of having a short URL. Detecting and\nresolving collisions also adds database lookups on insertion, introducing latency and\ncomplexity. This creates a tradeoff between uniqueness, shortness, and\nefficiency—making it difficult to optimize all three simultaneously.\n\n→ To handle collisions, implement a UNIQUE constraint on the short code column and\nretry with bounded attempts (e.g., max 3-5 retries) before falling back to a different\nstrategy or returning an error.\n→ Upon saving to the database, we'll get an error if the short code already exists. In this\ncase, we can simply retry the process with a random salt added to the hash function.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Unique Counter with Base62 Encoding\n\n→ Random number generator (RNG) to create short codes. Generates random number\neach time a new URL is shortened & used as short code\n→ Can use common RNG fns like JS's Math.random() or more robust cryptographic RNG\nfns for increased unpredictability [create note]\n→ RNGs don't provide enough entropy to ensure short codes are unique\n\n→ Use a hash function like SHA-256 to generate a fixed-size hash code instead\n→ Pure hash functions are deterministic: long URL always maps to same short code\nwithout needing to query DB\n→ May not be desirable (multiple codes per URL or want to prevent\nguessability/adversarial preimages), For latter, can add a secret salt or nonce (HMAC)\n→ Take the output and encode it using a base62 encoding scheme & take first N chars\nas short code. N is determined based on the number of characters needed to minimize\ncollisions (e.g., 8 characters gives 62^8 ≈ 218 trillion possible codes).\n\n\nChallenges\n→ There's still chance of generating duplicate short codes as the number of stored\nURLs increases.\n→ At large scale this can become non-negligible, requiring retries and database checks\nto enforce uniqueness\n\n→ To reduce collisions, we need higher entropy, which means generating longer short\ncodes. However, longer codes negate the benefit of having a short URL. Detecting and\nresolving collisions also adds database lookups on insertion, introducing latency and\ncomplexity. This creates a tradeoff between uniqueness, shortness, and\nefficiency—making it difficult to optimize all three simultaneously.\n\n→ To handle collisions, implement a UNIQUE constraint on the short code column and\nretry with bounded attempts (e.g., max 3-5 retries) before falling back to a different\nstrategy or returning an error.\n→ Upon saving to the database, we'll get an error if the short code already exists. In this\ncase, we can simply retry the process with a random salt added to the hash function.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can we ensure short urls are unique?\n\nRequirements:\nShort codes are unique.\nShort codes to be as short as possible\nShort codes are efficiently generated.\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Long Url Prefix\n\n→ Use a fixed prefix of the input url as the short code\n→ Take first N (eg: 8) characters of url and use as short code. www.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/  In this case www.short.ly/www.link\n→ We can maintain a PrecomputedFeed table & query from this instead of Follow and Post tables, for the GET /feeds\n\nChallenges\n→ Fails uniqueness constraint\nwww.linkedin.com/in/evan-king-40072280/ & www.linkedin.com/in/stefanmai/ both map to www.short.ly/www.link\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Hash Function\n\n→ Random number generator (RNG) to create short codes. Generates random number each time a new URL is shortened & used as short code\n→ Can use common RNG fns like JS's Math.random() or more robust cryptographic RNG fns for increased unpredictability [create note]\n→ RNGs don't provide enough entropy to ensure short codes are unique\n\n→ Use a hash function like SHA-256 to generate a fixed-size hash code instead\n→ Pure hash functions are deterministic: long URL always maps to same short code without needing to query DB\n→ May not be desirable (multiple codes per URL or want to prevent guessability/adversarial preimages), For latter, can add a secret salt or nonce (HMAC)\n→ Take the output and encode it using a base62 encoding scheme & take first N chars as short code. N is determined based on the number of characters needed to minimize collisions (e.g., 8 characters gives 62^8 ≈ 218 trillion possible codes).\n\n\nChallenges\n→ There's still chance of generating duplicate short codes as the number of stored URLs increases.\n→ At large scale this can become non-negligible, requiring retries and database checks to enforce uniqueness\n\n→ To reduce collisions, we need higher entropy, which means generating longer short codes. However, longer codes negate the benefit of having a short URL. Detecting and resolving collisions also adds database lookups on insertion, introducing latency and complexity. This creates a tradeoff between uniqueness, shortness, and efficiency—making it difficult to optimize all three simultaneously.\n\n→ To handle collisions, implement a UNIQUE constraint on the short code column and retry with bounded attempts (e.g., max 3-5 retries) before falling back to a different strategy or returning an error.\n→ Upon saving to the database, we'll get an error if the short code already exists. In this case, we can simply retry the process with a random salt added to the hash function.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Unique Counter with Base62 Encoding\n\n→ Random number generator (RNG) to create short codes. Generates random number each time a new URL is shortened & used as short code\n→ Can use common RNG fns like JS's Math.random() or more robust cryptographic RNG fns for increased unpredictability [create note]\n→ RNGs don't provide enough entropy to ensure short codes are unique\n\n→ Use a hash function like SHA-256 to generate a fixed-size hash code instead\n→ Pure hash functions are deterministic: long URL always maps to same short code without needing to query DB\n→ May not be desirable (multiple codes per URL or want to prevent guessability/adversarial preimages), For latter, can add a secret salt or nonce (HMAC)\n→ Take the output and encode it using a base62 encoding scheme & take first N chars as short code. N is determined based on the number of characters needed to minimize collisions (e.g., 8 characters gives 62^8 ≈ 218 trillion possible codes).\n\n\nChallenges\n→ There's still chance of generating duplicate short codes as the number of stored URLs increases.\n→ At large scale this can become non-negligible, requiring retries and database checks to enforce uniqueness\n\n→ To reduce collisions, we need higher entropy, which means generating longer short codes. However, longer codes negate the benefit of having a short URL. Detecting and resolving collisions also adds database lookups on insertion, introducing latency and complexity. This creates a tradeoff between uniqueness, shortness, and efficiency—making it difficult to optimize all three simultaneously.\n\n→ To handle collisions, implement a UNIQUE constraint on the short code column and retry with bounded attempts (e.g., max 3-5 retries) before falling back to a different strategy or returning an error.\n→ Upon saving to the database, we'll get an error if the short code already exists. In this case, we can simply retry the process with a random salt added to the hash function.",
