      "text": "2) How do we handle users with a large number of followers?\n\n\nUser has a large number of followers, similar fanout problem\nWhen we create a post: we need to write to millions of Feed records\nWe have upto 1 min to populate the feeds\n\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Blast the Requests\n\n→ Blast out all the requests at once from our Post Service when the post is created\n→ Worst case, have to write to millions of feeds with a new Post entry\n\nChallenges\n→ Single Post Service instance can only make limited network connections, as well as\nlatency supported by network card\n→ Best case, load on Post Service becomes incredibly uneven (one Post Service host\nmight be writing to millions of feeds while another is basically idle), making the system\ndifficult to scale.\n\nFor larger than normal posts, in order for us to consider it complete, at best we can\nparallelize the requests, but we still have to consolidate results, perform retries on\nfailures, and seperate genuine failed updates from false +ves, etc\n\nCelebrity posts, might even turn into serialized requests because of single instance\nresource limitations\nIt is generally best to decouple this complex handling out of a service that interacts\ndirectly with the user.\n\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Async Workers\n\n→ Use workers to update the feeds behind a queue that supports at-least-once delivery\n& scales well\n→ When new post is created we add an entry in the queue with post ID & creator user\nID.\n→ The worker will look up all followers for the creator and prepend the post to the front\nof their feeds\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Resource capabilities(network, CPU, memory) of workers will need to be very large\nto support celebrities\n→ The above concern could be alleviated. There will be variability in work to be done\nbetween tasks in the queue (1M followers vs 1000 followers).\n→ Strategies to alleviate;\n    → Break the feed update task into smaller chunks and add them to different queue for\nprocessing\n    → Use an external state service (like redis) to track completion of the task and find a\nway to leverage other workers in the cluster to share the load. Only update task\ncompletion when all chunks are done.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Async Workers with Hybrid Feeds\n\n→ Use the feed workers to add posts to feeds for non celebrity workers\n→ For celebs, instead of writing to followers feeds, we can add a flag to the Follow\ntable, to indicate not to precompute & workers will ignore these post requests \n→ when users request feed via Feed Service, we can get (partially) precomputed feed\nfrom Feed Table and merge with recent posts from celebrities that are followed (not\nprecomputed)\n→ Hybrid approach helps us fan out only for celebrity posts (smaller number) on read\npath and handle the rest of the load prior  \n\n\nChallenges\n→ Merging of feeds at read time vs at write time means more computation needs to be\ndone in the Feed Service\n→ Can be alleviated by having an account threshold during precomputing (maybe\nsmaller celebrities can be precomputed, etc)\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2) How do we handle users with a large number of followers?\n\n\nUser has a large number of followers, similar fanout problem\nWhen we create a post: we need to write to millions of Feed records\nWe have upto 1 min to populate the feeds\n\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: Blast the Requests\n\n→ Blast out all the requests at once from our Post Service when the post is created\n→ Worst case, have to write to millions of feeds with a new Post entry\n\nChallenges\n→ Single Post Service instance can only make limited network connections, as well as latency supported by network card\n→ Best case, load on Post Service becomes incredibly uneven (one Post Service host might be writing to millions of feeds while another is basically idle), making the system difficult to scale.\n\nFor larger than normal posts, in order for us to consider it complete, at best we can parallelize the requests, but we still have to consolidate results, perform retries on failures, and seperate genuine failed updates from false +ves, etc\n\nCelebrity posts, might even turn into serialized requests because of single instance resource limitations\nIt is generally best to decouple this complex handling out of a service that interacts directly with the user.\n\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Async Workers\n\n→ Use workers to update the feeds behind a queue that supports at-least-once delivery & scales well\n→ When new post is created we add an entry in the queue with post ID & creator user ID.\n→ The worker will look up all followers for the creator and prepend the post to the front of their feeds\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Resource capabilities(network, CPU, memory) of workers will need to be very large to support celebrities\n→ The above concern could be alleviated. There will be variability in work to be done between tasks in the queue (1M followers vs 1000 followers).\n→ Strategies to alleviate;\n    → Break the feed update task into smaller chunks and add them to different queue for processing\n    → Use an external state service (like redis) to track completion of the task and find a way to leverage other workers in the cluster to share the load. Only update task completion when all chunks are done.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Async Workers with Hybrid Feeds\n\n→ Use the feed workers to add posts to feeds for non celebrity workers\n→ For celebs, instead of writing to followers feeds, we can add a flag to the Follow table, to indicate not to precompute & workers will ignore these post requests \n→ when users request feed via Feed Service, we can get (partially) precomputed feed from Feed Table and merge with recent posts from celebrities that are followed (not precomputed)\n→ Hybrid approach helps us fan out only for celebrity posts (smaller number) on read path and handle the rest of the load prior  \n\n\nChallenges\n→ Merging of feeds at read time vs at write time means more computation needs to be done in the Feed Service\n→ Can be alleviated by having an account threshold during precomputing (maybe smaller celebrities can be precomputed, etc)\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3YCUGV7y8MJ4GcRq52F-n",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4702.085228280248,
      "y": 3907.0794759831206,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 1100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1000709334,
      "version": 3516,
      "versionNonce": 1937024053,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1768244583092,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3) How can we handle uneven reads of Posts?\n\n\n→ For the majority of posts, they will be read for few days, then never again\n→ Celebrity posts, number of reads in the first few hours will be massive\n→ DynamoDB, offers nearly infinite scaling provided there is even load across the\nkeyspace\n→ This traffic pattern causes Hot Key issue:\n→ A cache or database key that receives disproportionately high traffic compared to\nothers. Can create bottlenecks when traffic concentrates on a single shard or server.\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Post Cache with Large Keyspace\n\n→ We can use a distributed cache between readers of the Post table and itself\n→ As posts are far more likely to be created than edited, we can use large TTLs with an\nLRU eviction policy.\n→ We can obviously use database triggers to ensure cached post data stays consistent\non edits as well\n\nChallenges\n→ We have now moved the hot key problem from databse to cache\n→ Unlucky shard/partition that has multiple viral posts, will get an unequal distribution\nof load (again) which makes this cache very hard to scale\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Redundant Post Cache\n\n→ We can have multiple cache servers that readers can hit (these don't need to\ncordinate)\n→ By distributing read requests across the cache servers, multiple servers can all serve\nthe same postID\n→ This solution gives us N times the throughput for a hot key without any additional\ncoordination required\n→ A minor point to note, is that we make a minor tradeof- for a very viral post, with N\ncache instances, we might have N requests to the database instead of 1, which is much\nsmaller & manageable than millions of requests if there was no cache\n\nChallenges\n→ Because we replicate hot posts across cache instances, we store fewer unique posts\noverall, reducing cache storage efficiency\n→ But that’s acceptable since most posts are cold and DynamoDB can absorb\noccasional cache misses. The replication lets us scale read throughput for hot posts\nwithout coordination, which is the real goal",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3) How can we handle uneven reads of Posts?\n\n\n→ For the majority of posts, they will be read for few days, then never again\n→ Celebrity posts, number of reads in the first few hours will be massive\n→ DynamoDB, offers nearly infinite scaling provided there is even load across the keyspace\n→ This traffic pattern causes Hot Key issue:\n→ A cache or database key that receives disproportionately high traffic compared to others. Can create bottlenecks when traffic concentrates on a single shard or server.\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Post Cache with Large Keyspace\n\n→ We can use a distributed cache between readers of the Post table and itself\n→ As posts are far more likely to be created than edited, we can use large TTLs with an LRU eviction policy.\n→ We can obviously use database triggers to ensure cached post data stays consistent on edits as well\n\nChallenges\n→ We have now moved the hot key problem from databse to cache\n→ Unlucky shard/partition that has multiple viral posts, will get an unequal distribution of load (again) which makes this cache very hard to scale\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Redundant Post Cache\n\n→ We can have multiple cache servers that readers can hit (these don't need to cordinate)\n→ By distributing read requests across the cache servers, multiple servers can all serve the same postID\n→ This solution gives us N times the throughput for a hot key without any additional coordination required\n→ A minor point to note, is that we make a minor tradeof- for a very viral post, with N cache instances, we might have N requests to the database instead of 1, which is much smaller & manageable than millions of requests if there was no cache\n\nChallenges\n→ Because we replicate hot posts across cache instances, we store fewer unique posts overall, reducing cache storage efficiency\n→ But that’s acceptable since most posts are cold and DynamoDB can absorb occasional cache misses. The replication lets us scale read throughput for hot posts without coordination, which is the real goal",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
