    {
      "id": "hkCs3Ol4LAZmVjZIAM0N9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 896.2693693826636,
      "y": 4748.125629445211,
      "width": 812.7393188476562,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1744675483,
      "version": 1865,
      "versionNonce": 2126801872,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775236768010,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why base62? It's a compact representation of numbers that uses 62 characters (a-z, A-Z,\n0-9). The reason it's 62 and not the more common base64 is because we exclude + and / -\nthe slash is a path separator in URLs and the plus sign can be interpreted as a space in\nquery strings.\n\ninput_url = \"https://www.example.com/some/very/long/url\"\n# Canonicalize URL first (lowercase host, strip default ports, normalize trailing slash, etc.)\ncanonical_url = canonicalize(input_url)\nhash_code = hash_function(canonical_url)\nshort_code_encoded = base62_encode(hash_code)\nshort_code = short_code_encoded[:8] # 8 characters\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "y3IyYkIO3YRdtySwaxj2P",
      "originalText": "Why base62? It's a compact representation of numbers that uses 62 characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9). The reason it's 62 and not the more common base64 is because we exclude + and / - the slash is a path separator in URLs and the plus sign can be interpreted as a space in query strings.\n\ninput_url = \"https://www.example.com/some/very/long/url\"\n# Canonicalize URL first (lowercase host, strip default ports, normalize trailing slash, etc.)\ncanonical_url = canonicalize(input_url)\nhash_code = hash_function(canonical_url)\nshort_code_encoded = base62_encode(hash_code)\nshort_code = short_code_encoded[:8] # 8 characters\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_0Fxf1ZO26UossG_QxuOC",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 3592.6452618481594,
      "y": 3901.6468789966902,
      "width": 1007.5663329970248,
      "height": 3790.9305497014993,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1Y",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 1957896699,
      "version": 778,
      "versionNonce": 779408123,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "FsmaIecrKklS69PVcgK4R"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1768244579210,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "FsmaIecrKklS69PVcgK4R",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3597.6452618481594,
      "y": 3959.61215384744,
      "width": 993.87890625,
      "height": 3675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1Z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1066609307,
      "version": 1221,
      "versionNonce": 402045851,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1768244579210,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why do we choose not to precompute the feeds of the followers of celebrities in the last approach?\n\n\n1️⃣ What problem still exists even with async workers?\n\n→ Async workers + SQS fix connection limits and latency, but they do NOT fix work amplification.\n→ Let’s quantify:\n        Celebrity case\n        Followers = 90,000,000\n        1 post = 90,000,000 feed writes\n\n        Even with:\n            → 10,000 workers\n            → Perfect horizontal scaling\n\n→ You still must do 90 million writes. This causes three fundamental problems.\n\n\n2️⃣ Why this is still bad (even asynchronously)\n\n❌ Problem 1: Cost grows with followers (O(F))\n\n→ Fan-out on write cost:\n    Cost per post ∝ number of followers\n→ For celebrities:\n    → Write cost is enormous\n    → Happens for every post\n    → Independent of how many followers are active\n\n→ This violates a key scalability principle:\n    Work should scale with demand (reads), not potential demand (followers).\n\n❌ Problem 2: Uneven & bursty load (still)\n\n→ Even with a queue:\n    → One celebrity post enqueues millions of downstream writes\n    → That single post dominates the queue\n    → Other users’ feed updates get delayed\n\n→ This creates:\n    → Long-tail latency\n    → Priority inversion\n    → Hard-to-predict backlog spikes\n\nYou can shard jobs further, but…\n\n❌ Problem 3: The work is mostly wasted\n\n→ This is the killer argument.\n→ Most followers:\n    → Are offline\n    → Will never scroll far enough to see the post\n    → Don’t open the app in the consistency window\n→ Yet you eagerly paid:\n        90M writes × storage × replication\n\n📌 You’re computing feeds no one will read.\n\n\n3️⃣ Why precomputation works for normal users but not celebrities\n\n→ Normal user\n        Followers: 300\n        Reads per post: maybe 100\n        Writes: 300\n    acceptable tradeoff\n\n→ Celebrity\n        Followers: 90M\n        Reads per post (first hour): maybe 5M\n        Writes: 90M\n    terrible tradeoff\n\n\n→ So precomputation:\n        Works when follower count is small\n        Breaks when follower count is massive\n\n\n4️⃣ The key insight: demand-driven vs supply-driven work\n\n→ Fan-out on write = supply-driven\n        You push content everywhere just in case someone reads it.\n→ Fan-out on read = demand-driven\n        You compute only when: user opens feed\n→ For celebrities:\n        → Demand is high but distributed over time\n        → Naturally parallelizable\n        → Cacheable\n\n→ This makes fan-out on read inherently scalable.\n\n\n5️⃣ Why the hybrid approach is the right choice\n→ What we do instead\n        → Normal accounts → precompute feeds\n        → Celebrity accounts → do NOT precompute\n        → Mark follows as: isPrecomputed = false\n\n→ On write\n        Celebrity post → 1 write\n\n→ On read\n        User feed = precomputed feed + recent celebrity posts\n\n→ Now:\n        No write explosion\n        No queue flooding\n        No wasted writes\n\n6️⃣ Why this is fundamentally more scalable\n\nDimension                                Precompute celebrity feeds                        Hybrid\nWrites per post                        O(followers) ❌                                           O(1) ✅\nReads                                        Cheap                                                            Slightly more work\nCost aligns with usage           ❌                                                                   ✅\nHotspots                                   Yes                                                                 No\nQueue pressure                        Extreme                                                        Minimal\n\n📌 We are trading cheap writes for cheap reads — and that’s the correct trade at scale.\n\n7️⃣ Why “some inconsistency allowed” matters\n\n→ Because:\n        Celebrity posts are time-ordered\n        Slight delay or reordering is acceptable\n        Users already expect some lag\n\n→ This gives us freedom to:\n        Merge at read time\n        Cache aggressively\n        Relax strict consistency\n\n→ Without this NFR, the design would be much harder.\n\n8️⃣ One-sentence interview answer (memorize this)\n\n“We don’t precompute feeds for celebrities because fan-out on write scales with follower count, causing\nmassive, mostly wasted writes and queue hotspots. By switching celebrity content to fan-out on read, we\nmake the system demand-driven, cheaper, and far more scalable.”\n\n9️⃣ Interviewer follow-up you’re now ready for\n\nIf they ask: “Why not just add more workers?”\n\nYou answer: “Because the total amount of work doesn’t change — we’d still be doing tens of millions of writes\nper post, most of which are never read.”",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "_0Fxf1ZO26UossG_QxuOC",
      "originalText": "Why do we choose not to precompute the feeds of the followers of celebrities in the last approach?\n\n\n1️⃣ What problem still exists even with async workers?\n\n→ Async workers + SQS fix connection limits and latency, but they do NOT fix work amplification.\n→ Let’s quantify:\n        Celebrity case\n        Followers = 90,000,000\n        1 post = 90,000,000 feed writes\n\n        Even with:\n            → 10,000 workers\n            → Perfect horizontal scaling\n\n→ You still must do 90 million writes. This causes three fundamental problems.\n\n\n2️⃣ Why this is still bad (even asynchronously)\n\n❌ Problem 1: Cost grows with followers (O(F))\n\n→ Fan-out on write cost:\n    Cost per post ∝ number of followers\n→ For celebrities:\n    → Write cost is enormous\n    → Happens for every post\n    → Independent of how many followers are active\n\n→ This violates a key scalability principle:\n    Work should scale with demand (reads), not potential demand (followers).\n\n❌ Problem 2: Uneven & bursty load (still)\n\n→ Even with a queue:\n    → One celebrity post enqueues millions of downstream writes\n    → That single post dominates the queue\n    → Other users’ feed updates get delayed\n\n→ This creates:\n    → Long-tail latency\n    → Priority inversion\n    → Hard-to-predict backlog spikes\n\nYou can shard jobs further, but…\n\n❌ Problem 3: The work is mostly wasted\n\n→ This is the killer argument.\n→ Most followers:\n    → Are offline\n    → Will never scroll far enough to see the post\n    → Don’t open the app in the consistency window\n→ Yet you eagerly paid:\n        90M writes × storage × replication\n\n📌 You’re computing feeds no one will read.\n\n\n3️⃣ Why precomputation works for normal users but not celebrities\n\n→ Normal user\n        Followers: 300\n        Reads per post: maybe 100\n        Writes: 300\n    acceptable tradeoff\n\n→ Celebrity\n        Followers: 90M\n        Reads per post (first hour): maybe 5M\n        Writes: 90M\n    terrible tradeoff\n\n\n→ So precomputation:\n        Works when follower count is small\n        Breaks when follower count is massive\n\n\n4️⃣ The key insight: demand-driven vs supply-driven work\n\n→ Fan-out on write = supply-driven\n        You push content everywhere just in case someone reads it.\n→ Fan-out on read = demand-driven\n        You compute only when: user opens feed\n→ For celebrities:\n        → Demand is high but distributed over time\n        → Naturally parallelizable\n        → Cacheable\n\n→ This makes fan-out on read inherently scalable.\n\n\n5️⃣ Why the hybrid approach is the right choice\n→ What we do instead\n        → Normal accounts → precompute feeds\n        → Celebrity accounts → do NOT precompute\n        → Mark follows as: isPrecomputed = false\n\n→ On write\n        Celebrity post → 1 write\n\n→ On read\n        User feed = precomputed feed + recent celebrity posts\n\n→ Now:\n        No write explosion\n        No queue flooding\n        No wasted writes\n\n6️⃣ Why this is fundamentally more scalable\n\nDimension                                Precompute celebrity feeds                        Hybrid\nWrites per post                        O(followers) ❌                                           O(1) ✅\nReads                                        Cheap                                                            Slightly more work\nCost aligns with usage           ❌                                                                   ✅\nHotspots                                   Yes                                                                 No\nQueue pressure                        Extreme                                                        Minimal\n\n📌 We are trading cheap writes for cheap reads — and that’s the correct trade at scale.\n\n7️⃣ Why “some inconsistency allowed” matters\n\n→ Because:\n        Celebrity posts are time-ordered\n        Slight delay or reordering is acceptable\n        Users already expect some lag\n\n→ This gives us freedom to:\n        Merge at read time\n        Cache aggressively\n        Relax strict consistency\n\n→ Without this NFR, the design would be much harder.\n\n8️⃣ One-sentence interview answer (memorize this)\n\n“We don’t precompute feeds for celebrities because fan-out on write scales with follower count, causing massive, mostly wasted writes and queue hotspots. By switching celebrity content to fan-out on read, we make the system demand-driven, cheaper, and far more scalable.”\n\n9️⃣ Interviewer follow-up you’re now ready for\n\nIf they ask: “Why not just add more workers?”\n\nYou answer: “Because the total amount of work doesn’t change — we’d still be doing tens of millions of writes per post, most of which are never read.”",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
