      "seed": 1343247664,
      "version": 1699,
      "versionNonce": 162505168,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775236652812,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. What “fan-out” actually means\n\n→ One user request causes work to be performed across many independent entities or\nshards.\n→ The key is work expansion, not the number of SQL statements.\n\n→ So fan-out can happen:\n    → At the application layer\n    → At the database execution layer\n    → Across shards, partitions, or services\n\n\n2. “Because we can do this in one SQL query” — why that doesn’t eliminate fan-out\n\nSELECT *\nFROM posts\nWHERE author_id IN (\n  SELECT followee_id\n  FROM follows\n  WHERE follower_id = :user_id\n)\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n\n\n→ Even though it is one query, but internally this still fans out.\n→ If the system is scaled properly:\n    → follows table is sharded (by follower_id)\n    → posts table is sharded (by author_id or time)\n\n→ To execute that query, the database must:\n    → Read N followees\n    → For each followee, scan their posts\n    → Do this across many shards\n    → Merge + sort results\n\n→ So the fan-out moved inside the database engine.\n\n\n3. Fan-out is about data distribution, not syntax\n\n→ In terms of data ownership, Each followed user “owns” their own posts\n→ Those posts likely live:\n    → On different shards\n    → On different machines\n    → Possibly in different regions\n\n→ So fetching a feed means:\n1 request → N authors → N data partitions\n\nThat’s fan-out, regardless of the SQL query.\n\n\n4. Why fan-out is a problem (latency perspective)\n\n→ Latency is dictated by: slowest shard + merge time\n→ If a user follows:\n    10 users → fine\n    1,000 users → bad\n    10,000 users → catastrophic\n\n→ Even if:\n    → Queries are parallel\n    → Using a single SQL statement\n→ You still pay:\n    → Network cost\n    → CPU per shard\n    → Memory for merging\n    → Tail latency amplification\n\n\n5. Why FB News Feed is the classic fan-out example\n\nBecause:\n    → Users can follow millions of others\n    → Posts arrive continuously\n    → Feed must load in ~100 ms\n\nSo FB avoids read-time fan-out by:\n    → Fan-out on write (push model)\n    → Precomputing feeds\n    → Using timeline caches\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "mWw76F2uFewRT2naBn9Ii",
      "originalText": "1. What “fan-out” actually means\n\n→ One user request causes work to be performed across many independent entities or shards.\n→ The key is work expansion, not the number of SQL statements.\n\n→ So fan-out can happen:\n    → At the application layer\n    → At the database execution layer\n    → Across shards, partitions, or services\n\n\n2. “Because we can do this in one SQL query” — why that doesn’t eliminate fan-out\n\nSELECT *\nFROM posts\nWHERE author_id IN (\n  SELECT followee_id\n  FROM follows\n  WHERE follower_id = :user_id\n)\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n\n\n→ Even though it is one query, but internally this still fans out.\n→ If the system is scaled properly:\n    → follows table is sharded (by follower_id)\n    → posts table is sharded (by author_id or time)\n\n→ To execute that query, the database must:\n    → Read N followees\n    → For each followee, scan their posts\n    → Do this across many shards\n    → Merge + sort results\n\n→ So the fan-out moved inside the database engine.\n\n\n3. Fan-out is about data distribution, not syntax\n\n→ In terms of data ownership, Each followed user “owns” their own posts\n→ Those posts likely live:\n    → On different shards\n    → On different machines\n    → Possibly in different regions\n\n→ So fetching a feed means:\n1 request → N authors → N data partitions\n\nThat’s fan-out, regardless of the SQL query.\n\n\n4. Why fan-out is a problem (latency perspective)\n\n→ Latency is dictated by: slowest shard + merge time\n→ If a user follows:\n    10 users → fine\n    1,000 users → bad\n    10,000 users → catastrophic\n\n→ Even if:\n    → Queries are parallel\n    → Using a single SQL statement\n→ You still pay:\n    → Network cost\n    → CPU per shard\n    → Memory for merging\n    → Tail latency amplification\n\n\n5. Why FB News Feed is the classic fan-out example\n\nBecause:\n    → Users can follow millions of others\n    → Posts arrive continuously\n    → Feed must load in ~100 ms\n\nSo FB avoids read-time fan-out by:\n    → Fan-out on write (push model)\n    → Precomputing feeds\n    → Using timeline caches\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
